   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Summary (2026-10-12)\n\n**States.** All HW cyclic joint eigenstates of t̄₁, t̄₃ at the ABJM point: 6 (L = 2), 26 (L = 3), 152 (L = 4 pairs); all H_MZ eigenstates (≤ 3.5·10⁻¹³), joint spectrum non-degenerate (gap ≥ 0.0065). Saved in `data/abjm-hw-states.wxf`.\n\n**Two exact Z₂'s of the ABJM chain** (both commute with H_MZ):\n- 𝒫 = Weyl∘(one-site shift): 𝒫 t̄₁(u) 𝒫⁻¹ = t̄₃(u+h) — the analogue of 2603's LR map (4 ↔ 4̄). LR-symmetric ⇔ τ̄₁(u) = τ̄₃(u+h).\n- 𝒬 = (signed Weyl)∘(reflection): 𝒬 t̄₁(u) 𝒬⁻¹ = t̄₁(2h−u) — parity. Every 4-pair state is either a 𝒬-eigenstate with eigenvalue (−1)^p or part of a 𝒬-pair (q = 0).\n\n**When does C_ℓ = ⟨B_ℓ|Ψ⟩ vanish** (L = 2, 3, 4 pairs, all 184 states):\n1. **R-symmetry:** C_ℓ = 0 for all ℓ unless Ψ ∈ [p,0,p] (the bra only has e₁, e₄ components, so w₂ = w₃ = 0); and only L−p ≤ ℓ ≤ L+p.\n2. **Bridge parity (kinematic):** if L+p is odd, C_ℓ = 0 unless ℓ ≡ L+p (mod 2) — for every state in the sector (LR or not, 𝒬-symmetric or paired), so the bra has no component along the whole HW cyclic [p,0,p] subspace for those ℓ. If L+p is even, both parities occur.\n3. **No LR selection rule:** unlike 2603 (C = 0 unless τ₊ = τ₋), non-LR states (τ̄₁(u) ≠ τ̄₃(u+h)) have nonzero C_ℓ, e.g. the L = 2 adjoint pair |C₁| = 1/√12, the L = 3 [1,0,1] pair at E = 6.456 (ℓ = 2, 3, 4). 𝒬-pairs also have nonzero C. The bra B_ℓ is not 𝒫-covariant (Π moves the bridge to start on an F site), so the 2603 mechanism ⟨W|(t₊ − t₋) = 0 is not available.\n4. **Isolated dynamical zeros at L = 4:** the LR [1,0,1] state at E = 32 has C_ℓ = 0 for all ℓ; the LR [2,0,2] state at E = 8 (𝒫 = −1) has only the even bridges {2, 4, 6}. Not explained by 𝒫 or 𝒬 (other LR states with the same eigenvalues are nonzero); both have integer energy (candidates for singular / exceptional Bethe states).\n\nOpen: whether the physical ABJM structure constant should use a 𝒫-symmetrised bra (both bridge placements, starting on A or F) — then the LR question changes; twisted version (2603 works at generic twist with ω = 1).",
